 { There are many different ways to fight against your opponent's control of the centre. While it is not possible to study all the ways in which you can do so, in this section we'll cover a selection of the most popular methods. We'll look at one example of each of the following. 1. Pawn breaks 2. Punishing time-wasting 3. Counterplay along the flanks 4. Damaging the opponent's structure 5. Forcing exchanges 6. Targeting the base of the pawn chain Let's turn to our first example now, on pawn breaks! Here, White's pawn on d4 offers them superior central control for now. However, Black has a move that can take advantage of one of the downsides of the opponent's position, namely that their queen is undefended. Can you find the best way to play as Black? } 1... { -KEY- } e5 $1 { The best pawn break! ...c5 instead would also be a way of challenging the centre, but would be much less convincing since it would open up the h1-a8 diagonal, playing into White's hands as their fianchettoed bishop on g2 would gain in strength. } ( { If Black is careless and plays a normal-looking move such as } 1... Nf6 { then White will retreat their rook and their extra space will become a problem for Black over time. } 2. Re1 Bg4 3. c3 $14 { White has a small but stable advantage. } ) 
2. dxe5 { White grabs a pawn, but now Black's tactical point is revealed. } ( 2. c3 { White can try to hang on to the centre, but a single pawn exchange is enough for Black to neutralise White's dream of a strong centre. Play might continue: } exd4 3. Nxd4 ( 3. cxd4 $15 { would hang on to the centre, but only at the cost of leaving White with an isolated queen's pawn. At the same time, after the move ...Nf6 Black would be ready to occupy the d5 outpost in the future. White cannot take this approach as Black would be the one left with the better position. } ) 
Nf6 4. Re1 Bg4 $11 { Black has managed to find decent squares for all of their pieces, and given the symmetrical pawn structure, the position is equal. } ) 
Nxe5 $1 { Correct! Without this shot, Black would simply be a pawn down. However, with the queen being undefended White is not in time to win Black's knight. } 3. Qxd8 Nxf3+ ( 3... Rxd8 { Black could even ignore the knight for one more move, since after } 4. Nxe5 $4 Rd1 $1 5. Bf1 Bh3 $19 { White is lost due to the threat of mate. } ) 
4. Bxf3 Rxd8 5. c3 { Shutting down the influence of Black's fianchettoed bishop. } Be6 $11 { Black has completely solved their opening problems. Pawn breaks will often be essential to undermine a central advantage, and can often be supported by tactics as in this example. } 
