 { In the following example, we'll discuss time wasting by the side with the greater central control. It's crucial to stay vigilant for your opponent's unproductive moves, as these can degrade their position relative to yours, opening up various opportunities. Let's take a look at a game from the beginning, where White grabs a bigger slice of the centre than Black but follows this up with unhelpful pawn moves on each flank of the board. } 1. { -KEY- } e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. c4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. Bf4 Bg7 { After only six moves, Black already has no problems. White may have two pawns in the centre, but they still have to deploy both of their minor pieces on the kingside before they will be able to castle. } 7. c5 $6 { White grabs some space on the central flanks, but it's a dangerous strategy given their lag in development. } O-O 8. h3 $2 { The first major inaccuracy. White could not afford to spend any more time on pawn moves. } ( { Their concern must have been that if } 8. Nf3 { then after } Bg4 $15 { Black would be able to pin the knight and in doing so make it more challenging for White to defend their d4 pawn. This is true, but the cure turns out to be worse than the disease. } ) 
Nc6 { Black continues to play sensible developing moves, and by now has amassed a significant lead in development. } 9. a3 $2 { A losing mistake, as White burns yet another tempo on an unnecessary pawn move. } Ne4 $1 { Black is ready for an all-out confrontation between pieces, and is the first to cross into the enemy camp. The threat is to take on d4 next. } 10. Nf3 { Defending the pawn. } Nxd4 $1 { Unsurprisingly, Black already has a powerful tactical shot! } 11. Nxd4 e5 { Black will regain the piece, and the fact that the centre has blasted open can only favour the more developed side. } 12. Be3 exd4 $1 13. Bxd4 Re8 14. Be2 { White has to cover the e-file. } Qg5 $1 { Game over. There is no good way to defend g2. } 15. g4 { Necessary, but now White's dark squares are left horribly weak. } ( 15. O-O $2 { would lose a piece after } Bxd4 16. Qxd4 Nxc3 17. bxc3 Rxe2 $19 ) 
 ( { After } 15. Kf1 $2 Nd2 16. Kg1 Bxd4 { again Black wins a piece. } ) 
Qh4 ( { The threat is } 15... Qh4  Bxg4 { since } 17. hxg4 { would lose to } Qxh1 { while } 17. Bxg4 { would be met by } Nxc3 { . } ) 
16. Kf1 Bd7 $19 { Black plays a quiet developing move, with the intention of bringing the bishop to c6 next. White's entire position is so riddled with holes that it is only a matter of time before Black finds a decisive breakthrough. White's loss here did not have so much to do with overextension, but rather the moves that followed. It costs time to grab space, whether that's in the centre or elsewhere on the board. Therefore, following up a space-grab with time-wasting will almost always lead to disaster. } 
