 { The third method of fighting against the centre is to generate counterplay on a different part of the board, commonly the queenside. Since in this case, Black does not have an attractive pawn break (the move ...e6 would compromise their structure), they are forced to take a different approach. } 1... { -KEY- } c4 $1 { Not the only move, but it makes sense to gain time against the bishop while creating a potential outpost on d3, as well as freeing down the c5 square for the knight. } 2. Bc2 Nc5 { Targeting the outpost. } ( 2... Qb6 { was another strong approach, in this case targeting the weak squares on f2 and e3, the product of White's pawn advances. } 3. Kh1 Ng4 4. Qe1 Ne3 $1 5. Bxe3 Qxe3 { Black has captured one of White's most important pieces, the dark-squared bishop. This hurts White a lot since their dark squares are very compromised. The position is already slightly better for Black in spite of remaining a pawn down. } ) 
3. h3 { Preventing any ...Ng4 ideas. } Nd3 $1 4. Rb1 { Protecting b2 makes sense, as this pawn lies on the semi-open b-file. } ( 4. Bxd3 $2 cxd3 5. Ng3 d2 $17 { winning the exchange. } ) 
Nd7 { Increasing the scope of the dark-squared bishop and looking to introduce the other knight into the action. } 5. Be3 Rfb8 6. Kh2 { White has no good way of defending the b2 pawn, and they could not have advanced it since the knight on c3 would have been lost. } Rxb2 $11 { The continued pressure leads to Black recovering their pawn, with a position that the computer evaluates as equal but in practical terms seems much easier to play for Black due to their superior piece activity. Black's strategy was successful because they were able to play around the centre and generate strong piece pressure on the queenside. Another approach you can take when fighting against the centre is to target your opponent's structure, as in the next example. } 
