 { In this position, Black has only one path to equality. Can you find the best 2-move sequence? } 1... { -KEY- } Bxf3 $1 { Seizing the opportunity to damage the structure. } ( 1... Nxc3 { This is the next best option, since at least here Black would remove a pair of minor pieces from the board. However, after } 2. Qxc3 $16 { the space advantage would continue to undermine Black's piece activity, as well as the lingering threat of pushing d4-d5 when Black could find themselves being steamrolled. } ) 
 ( { Jumping forward immediately with } 1... Nf4 $2 { would allow White to keep a healthy advantage with the move } 2. Ne2 $16 { White is well-placed against Black's piece pressure on the kingside while their powerful centre will be a major problem in the long run. } ) 
2. gxf3 Nf4 $1 { Correct! The damage to the structure has left a weak square on f4 that allows Black active play in spite of their poor grip on the centre. } 3. Ne2 { Defending d4 and targeting the invading piece. } ( 3. d5 { is risky for White, as it loses control over the e5 square. } Ne5 4. Ne2 Nxe2 5. Bxe2 Ng6 $11 { Black has no problems here, with the weak f4 square comfortably offsetting any advantage White might hope to have due to the bishop pair. } ) 
Nxe2+ $1 { Another set of pieces gets exchanged. As a position simplifies, the influence of the space advantage tends to be reduced. } 4. Bxe2 Bf6 $1 { Black continues to play against the dark squares, as they are the weaker color complex. } 5. d5 { Black also has no problems if White advances the pawn centre along the dark squares instead: } ( 5. Qe3 Rfd8 6. e5 Be7 $11 { Black is by no means worse here, since they have gained an outpost on d5 while the locked central structure is not helpful for White's bishops. The position is unclear, but approximately balanced. } ) 
Bxb2 6. dxc6 { Black has seen that he will not lose a piece here. } ( 6. Qxb2 $6 { Avoiding the forcing sequence with this move is not advisable. } Ne7 7. Qb3 e5 $13 { White has to be careful to avoid such paths, since it's very uncertain that White's advanced centre is a more valuable asset than Black's outpost on f4 and attacking prospects. } ) 
bxc6 $1 7. Rxd7 ( { If } 7. Qxb8 { then } Qxd1 $15 { when Black gets a better version of the ending reached in the game. } ) 
Rxb3 8. axb3 Bxa1 9. Rxc7 Rb8 10. Rxc6 Rxb3 11. Rxa6 Be5 $11 { The extra pawn is not significant here given the presence of opposite colored bishops. In this example, Black was able to fully solve their problems in the centre by inflicting damage to the opponent's structure and then generating play based on the newly created weaknesses. Let's now conclude the section by checking out two more methods that should already be familiar: seeking out exchanges, and targeting the base of a pawn chain. } 
