 { We can see that here White has the greater central control, and is now using this superiority as a springboard to launch an attack on the kingside. However, Black is doing fine here so long as they do not panic and make sure to seek out piece exchanges at the right moment. } 1... { -KEY- } Be7 $1 { A somewhat mysterious move, but a very clever one. Rather than lashing out, Black arranges their pieces ideally to prepare to meet the g4-g5 advance. } ( 1... Ne8 $6 { You must be careful to avoid panicked play when facing a monster knight on e5. Here Black starts by retreating their knight in order to set up the move ...f6, desperate to kick back the invader. } 2. g5 f6 $4 { Following through on the mistaken plan. } 3. Bxh7 $1 Kxh7 4. Qh5 Kg8 5. g6 $18 { Black is getting mated. Perhaps you recognise this pattern, as I suspect there are very few chess players on the planet who have not lost in this fashion when facing systems where White clamps a knight on the e5 square. Or at least I can say I have lost in this way more times than I care to remember! } ) 
2. g5 Nxe5 $1 { The first piece exchange. } 3. dxe5 { The most challenging approach, since it appears that Black's ...Ne4 idea is no longer possible. } ( 3. fxe5 $6 { This move is not so challenging against Black's idea, since here the g5 pawn is loose. } Ne4 $1 { White is not in time to win a pawn on e4, because the g5 pawn would fall. } 4. Nxe4 dxe4 5. Bxe4 Bxe4 6. Qxe4 Bxg5 $15 { After the flurry of exchanges, White's attack on the kingside has ground to a halt while the space advantage is no longer so significant. Black also has the safer king and the more active pieces. } ) 
Ne4 $1 { Black plays it anyway! Suddenly we start to see the subtle idea behind dropping the bishop back to e7. } 4. Nxe4 dxe4 5. Bxe4 Bxe4 6. Qxe4 { Black has given up a pawn, but now they control the d-file... And because of Black's earlier move (1...Be7) their queen is immediately ready to exert influence along it. } c4 $1 { Grabbing space on the queenside and securing an outpost on d3. Black understands that White needs to fight for the open file in order to bring their pieces into the game. Play might continue: } 7. Qf3 Qd3 8. e4 { White has to challenge the Black queen, and Rd1 instead was not convincing after ...Qc2 when the queen would continue to frustrate White's development. } Bc5+ 9. Kh1 Qc2 10. Qg2 Qd3 11. Qf3 $11 { With a draw by repetition. The problem for Black is that it's hard to claim an advantage in the queenless endgame, while White cannot hope to develop their pieces with the Black queen on the board. In my view, this way of handling the position by Black was deeply instructive. If you've ever struggled to handle an invading central knight, it may be worth reviewing the ideas contained in this game. For our final example of the section, we'll be revisiting a technique previously discussed in the chapter on 'Space'. } 
