 { Here we return to the theme of overextension, which was examined across several examples in the 'Fighting against Space' section of the previous chapter. } 1... { -KEY- } Nh6 $1 { Black develops around the space constraints, eyeing up the f5 square for their knight. From here, the intention is to pressure the base of White's central pawn chain on d4. } 2. Bd3 Bg4 $1 { Black shows a second idea in the attack against the d4 pawn. The threat is ...Bxf3 next, when the d4 pawn would be undefended. } ( 2... Nf5 $6 { The immediate targeting of the pawn would be less effective, since after } 3. Bb5 $11 { White uses the pin against the knight to keep the d4 pawn stable. } ) 
3. Be3 { White has to step back to safeguard the centre. } ( 3. Nbd2 $2 { Defending the knight would not work, since Black can take the pawn immediately: } Nxd4 $17 ) 
O-O 4. O-O f6 $1 { Black turns to pawn breaks as a way of making further progress. } ( 4... Nf5 { would not be a bad move, but after } 5. Bxf5 Bxf5 $11 { the position is roughly even as White's superior central control is counter-balanced by Black's strong bishop pair. } ) 
5. exf6 ( { The exchange of pawns with } 5. Bxg6 $2 fxe5 $19 { would lead to disaster for White because of the enormous pressure against the knight on f3. } ) 
Rxf6 6. Be2 { Yet another defensive move is needed in order to avoid their structure being ruined. } Bxf3 7. Bxf3 Nf5 8. Nc3 Rd6 $17 { Black has their weaknesses under control, while White's d4 pawn can no longer be saved. The result is that no matter what White tries, they will lose a pawn in the next couple of moves. For example: } 9. Bf4 Rd7 10. g3 Bxd4 $17 { In this example, White was punished as they had built up an advanced centre that could not sustain the pressure of Black's well-coordinated minor pieces. This concludes the section, good job working your way through it! These methods should provide you with a strong toolkit to counter strategies or openings that have central control as their aim. For our final section of the chapter, we'll flip perspective and speak about ways in which you can protect the centre if you are the side with the advantage. } 
